course: technology
lesson: 41
title: Rivers, canals and boats: locks, weirs, water-mills, barges and how not to tip a rowing boat
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: In «The Wind in the Willows» the Rat sculls his little boat on the river, the Mole tips it over by grabbing the oars, Portly goes missing near a roaring weir, and Toad rides on a canal barge pulled by a horse. All of these are real machines and real places that people built and used.
explain: A canal is a river made by people. About 250 years ago Britain dug thousands of kilometres of canals, because a single horse walking on the towpath could pull a boat carrying far more coal than it could pull in a cart on a bumpy road.
explain: Canals cannot run uphill, so engineers built locks: boxes of water with gates at each end that lift a boat up or let it down like a water lift, with no pump at all, only water flowing downhill.
explain: Rivers were tamed too: weirs keep the water deep enough for boats and drive water-mills, but the churning water below a weir is one of the most dangerous places to swim.
explain: So the river bank in the book is full of engineering, if you know where to look.
fact: A canal is a waterway dug by people; Britain's canal age ran from about 1760 to the 1830s, starting with the Bridgewater Canal of 1761, which carried coal to Manchester and made it much cheaper.
fact: A horse that could pull about 2 tonnes in a cart on a road could pull a canal boat carrying about 30 tonnes, because water holds the boat up and there are no bumps.
fact: The horse walked along the towpath beside the canal, pulling the boat by a long rope, while someone on the boat steered with the tiller, a long handle at the back, just like the barge-woman in the book.
fact: Narrowboats are only about 2 metres wide, to fit the narrow locks, and up to about 21 metres long; many boat families lived in a tiny cabin at the back, painted with roses and castles.
fact: To take a boat up through a lock: it goes in, the bottom gates are shut, small sluices in the top gates let water in, the boat rises, and when the water is level the top gates open; the water that flows in is the lock's length times its width times how far the water rises, in cubic metres.
fact: A lock needs no pump: water simply flows down from the higher part of the canal, and reservoirs keep the top of the canal full.
fact: From the 1840s railways took most of the canals' trade, and today about 2,000 miles of canals and rivers in England and Wales are used mostly for holidays, walking and fishing, with a speed limit of 4 miles an hour on canals.
fact: A weir is a low wall across a river that keeps the water deep upstream for boats and can send water to a mill; below it the water churns round and round and can hold a swimmer under, so never swim near a weir.
fact: A water-mill uses moving water to turn a wheel, which turns heavy millstones that grind grain into flour; the Domesday Book of 1086 counted more than 5,000 mills in England.
fact: Sculling means one person rows with two oars called sculls, as the Rat does; in rowing each person holds one oar with both hands; both face backwards, because pulling the oars towards you is the strongest way to row, while a canoeist with a paddle faces forwards.
fact: A punt is a flat-bottomed boat pushed along with a long pole on shallow rivers; a painter is the rope at the front of a boat used to tie it up.
fact: Small boats tip over easily if someone stands up or lunges to one side, as the Mole did when he grabbed the sculls; you step into the middle, keep low, sit still and wear a life jacket.
fact: Fast boats make waves called wash that wear away river banks, which is one reason for the canal speed limit and why the Rat shouted at the steam-launches.
exercise: What is a canal? | A waterway dug by people | A river that flows backwards | A lake high in the mountains | A road made of planks
exercise: What did the Bridgewater Canal of 1761 carry? | Coal to Manchester | Tea from faraway China | Soldiers off to France | Fresh fish to London
exercise: Why could a horse pull far more on a canal than on a road? | Water holds the boat up and there are no bumps | Canal horses were bigger than all road horses | The canal water flows downhill all the way | Boats have engines that help the horse along
exercise: Where did the canal horse walk? | Along the towpath beside the canal | In the shallow water of the canal itself | On the roof of the narrowboat it pulled | Along the railway line next to it
exercise: How wide is a narrowboat? | About 2 metres | About 20 metres | Only about 1 metre | About 10 metres
exercise: What lifts a boat in a lock? | Water let in through sluices from higher up | A big electric pump hidden under the boat | A tall crane that picks the boat up | Horses pulling the boat up a steep ramp
exercise: What happens first when a boat goes up through a lock? | It goes in and the bottom gates are shut | The top gates are opened while it waits outside | The water is pumped out of the lock | The boat is lifted onto rollers
exercise: Why do canals need locks? | Water cannot flow uphill, so boats need steps of water | Locks keep thieves away from the boats tied up at night | Locks stop the fish from swimming too far away from home | Locks make the water warmer for swimming in
exercise: What took most of the canals' trade from the 1840s? | Railways | Aeroplanes | Motor lorries | Bicycle couriers
exercise: What is the speed limit on canals? | 4 miles an hour | 40 miles an hour | 20 miles an hour | There is no speed limit
exercise: What does a weir do? | Keeps the water deep upstream | Lifts boats up and down | Catches fish for the miller | Cleans the river water of mud
exercise: Why should you never swim near a weir? | The churning water below can hold a swimmer under | The water near a weir is always too cold to swim in safely | Weirs are full of pike that bite | The weir wall might fall on you
exercise: What does a water-mill grind? | Grain into flour | Stones into fine sand | Wood into paper pulp | Coal into black dust
exercise: About how many mills did the Domesday Book of 1086 count? | More than 5,000 | Exactly 12 | About 100 | More than a million
exercise: What is sculling? | One person rowing with two oars | Two people rowing with one oar each | Pushing a boat along with a long pole | Steering a barge with a tiller
exercise: What is a boat's painter? | The rope at the front used to tie it up | The person who paints the boat's name on it | The small sail at the back of the boat | The long pole used to push a punt
exercise: What is wash? | Waves made by a fast boat | Soap used to clean a boat | Rain that fills a boat | A kind of river weed
test: «canals are just rivers innit» What is right? | A canal is dug by people, while a river is natural | Yes, canals and rivers are exactly the same thing with a different name | A canal is a river that has been frozen solid | A canal is a river that only flows at night
test: A Victorian boatman says one horse on the towpath does the work of fifteen horses with carts. How can that be? | A horse can pull about 30 tonnes by boat but about 2 tonnes by cart | Canal horses ate fifteen times as much food as other horses | The boat had fifteen more horses hidden inside it | It was a lie, as boats are much harder to pull than carts
test: In the book Toad offers to steer the barge instead of washing. What steers a barge? | The tiller at the back | The horse's reins | The oars at the front | A steering wheel on the roof
test: «how does a lock lift a whole boat with no engine» What is the best answer? | Water flows in from the higher canal and the boat floats up with it | A man winds the boat up with a big handle and a heavy chain | The boat's own engine climbs straight up the walls | Magnets under the water push the boat up slowly
test: Portly goes missing and the Otter fears the weir. Why is a weir dangerous for a small swimmer? | The churning water below it can hold a swimmer under | The water above a weir is always boiling hot in summer | Weirs suck everything up into the air | Weirs are electric
test: «we're going on a narrowboat holiday, can we go 30 mph to get there quicker?» What is right? | No, the canal speed limit is 4 miles an hour, to protect the banks | Yes, narrowboats are built to go as fast as cars on the motorway | Yes, but only at night when the canal is empty of boats | No, the limit is 3 miles a day, to save fuel
test: The Mole grabs the sculls, lunges and «Sploosh! Over went the boat». What should you do in a small boat? | Step into the middle, keep low and sit still | Stand up and wave the oars to keep your balance | Jump from side to side to test it | Lean right out over one side
test: A painting shows a narrowboat cabin covered in roses and castles. Who lived there? | Often a whole boat family | Nobody, it was only for coal | The horse | The lock-keeper's cat
test: «the rat rows facing backwards?? thats dumb» Why do rowers and scullers face backwards? | They pull the oars towards themselves, which is the strongest way to row | They are too scared to see where they are going | It is just a silly rule invented for races in Oxford | They want to see the people behind them on the bank
test: A friend says the old mill wheel turned because a horse walked round and round it. What really turned a water-mill? | Moving water pushing the wheel | A horse walking round it | Wind in its four sails | A steam engine under it
test: ★★★ A lock is 20 metres long and 2 metres wide, and the water rises 3 metres to lift a boat. How many cubic metres of water flow in? | 120 | 60 | 25 | 1200
exam: Identify the principal advantage of conveying heavy goods by canal rather than by road in the eighteenth century. | A single horse could draw a far greater load upon the water | Canals were open to traffic only at night, when the roads were closed | Boats required no persons whatsoever to steer them | Road carts were forbidden by law
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the operation of a canal lock. | Water admitted from the higher level raises the vessel, no pump being required | The vessel is raised by an engine of great power concealed beneath the lock | The vessel is hauled up a ramp by a team of horses | The water is boiled until it rises
exam: Ascertain the reason for which bathing in the vicinity of a weir is to be avoided. | The circulating water below the weir may hold a swimmer beneath the surface | The water above the weir is invariably polluted by the mill | Weirs are inhabited by dangerous eels which bite any bather they encounter | Bathing is permitted only on Sundays
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the practice of sculling? | A single person propels the boat with a pair of oars | Two persons propel the boat with a single oar between them | The boat is propelled by a pole upon the river bed | The boat is drawn by a horse upon the bank
